  "description": "I'm confused about how we validate science now",
  "body_html": "<p><em>Thank you to <a href=\"https://unseriously-serious.com/\">Arina Berezovsky</a> for the many discussions and ideas that formed this post. </em></p><p>Suppose that Bob, in 50 A.D, observes that his oranges fall to the ground every time he cuts them from a branch of his orange tree. He then grows another orange tree to see what happens when he cuts them, and lo and behold, they also fall to the ground. Bob thinks for a while and makes a theory that ALL oranges, once cut from trees, fall to the ground.</p><p>Now, Bob tells Alice of his findings through the village publisher. Alice tries to verify this theory by growing her own orange tree. She cuts a branch. And lo and behold, the orange falls to the ground. </p><p>Has Alice successfully replicated Bob\u2019s experiment, even though she used a different orange tree? Even if she possibly grew it with different soil? What if Alice\u2019s orange fruit fell down at a different speed, or were a different species? </p><p>Is Bob\u2019s theory more plausible as a result of Alice\u2019s replication? </p><p>I dunno\u2026</p><p>Furthermore, suppose Catherine from the other side of the village starts waxing lyrical about all sour fruits falling to the floor. And then Donald decides to grow a lemon tree and finds that his fruits also fall to the floor when he cuts them. Has Donald validated Catherines general theory of citrus fruits? </p><p>Is the general theory of sour fruit falling thingies more valid?!!</p><p>I also dunno\u2026</p><div class=\"captioned-image-container\"><figure><a class=\"image-link image2 is-viewable-img\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!hbfc!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F29b060d4-9082-4161-af1a-f7a6bc9f1880_4032x3024.jpeg\" data-component-name=\"Image2ToDOM\"><div class=\"image2-inset\"><picture><source type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!hbfc!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F29b060d4-9082-4161-af1a-f7a6bc9f1880_4032x3024.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!hbfc!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F29b060d4-9082-4161-af1a-f7a6bc9f1880_4032x3024.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!hbfc!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F29b060d4-9082-4161-af1a-f7a6bc9f1880_4032x3024.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!hbfc!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F29b060d4-9082-4161-af1a-f7a6bc9f1880_4032x3024.jpeg 1456w\" sizes=\"100vw\"><img src=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!hbfc!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F29b060d4-9082-4161-af1a-f7a6bc9f1880_4032x3024.jpeg\" width=\"4032\" height=\"3024\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/29b060d4-9082-4161-af1a-f7a6bc9f1880_4032x3024.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:3024,&quot;width&quot;:4032,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:null,&quot;bytes&quot;:1583821,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true,&quot;topImage&quot;:false,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:&quot;https://chillphysicsenjoyer.substack.com/i/207814719?img=https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc0a756d6-cc90-46f2-ac95-439796b60f31_4032x3024.jpeg&quot;,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}\" class=\"sizing-normal\" alt=\"\" srcset=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!hbfc!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F29b060d4-9082-4161-af1a-f7a6bc9f1880_4032x3024.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!hbfc!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F29b060d4-9082-4161-af1a-f7a6bc9f1880_4032x3024.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!hbfc!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F29b060d4-9082-4161-af1a-f7a6bc9f1880_4032x3024.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!hbfc!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F29b060d4-9082-4161-af1a-f7a6bc9f1880_4032x3024.jpeg 1456w\" sizes=\"100vw\" loading=\"lazy\"></picture><div class=\"image-link-expand\"><div class=\"pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset\"><button tabindex=\"0\" type=\"button\" class=\"pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image buttonBase-GK1x3M\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 20 20\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke=\"var(--color-fg-primary)\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" class=\"icon-noB79L\"><g><path d=\"M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882\"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex=\"0\" type=\"button\" class=\"pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image buttonBase-GK1x3M\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2 icon-noB79L\"><polyline points=\"15 3 21 3 21 9\"></polyline><polyline points=\"9 21 3 21 3 15\"></polyline><line x1=\"21\" x2=\"14\" y1=\"3\" y2=\"10\"></line><line x1=\"3\" x2=\"10\" y1=\"21\" y2=\"14\"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a><figcaption class=\"image-caption\">Uncalibrated thermometers at the Galileo museum in Florence. </figcaption></figure></div><div><hr></div><p>Scientists do experiments. From the result of these experiments, they make general statements about the world called theories. One way to check whether these theories hold any weight is to replicate their experiments. </p><p>But what is the goal of replication? </p><p>Supposedly the goal of a replication it is to build confidence that a theory actually describes the world to some degree of (rough) precision. </p><p>But if you take this as the goal, then it\u2019s <strong>actually unclear how strict a replication needs to be to still be useful to accomplish this goal</strong>. A true replication of an experiment would be to do something with exactly the same conditions, but this is impossible, because different labs are in different locations and times.  </p><p>And so if we can give up on the impossible goal of doing exact replications, how do we define the boundaries of what experiments count as valid replications of a theory? It feels like the possibilities are wide. On one hand, you could try replicate everything about an experiment except for its time and place. And on the other hand you could just do what Alice did and try your best to capture the essential points. </p><p>It\u2019s unclear what is the best way! </p><div><hr></div><p>In this blog I\u2019ve done experiments related to theories or phenomena that I\u2019ve suspected to be true. </p><p>Some of this has been stuff in the physical world that I can see or measure myself. For example, while I haven\u2019t tried to replicate strict papers on UV killing yeast, I\u2019ve done experiments that demonstrate the same <strong>effect</strong>. Does this count as a valid piece of science? </p><p>I\u2019ve also tried to replicate and measure the claimed air cleaning rates of  air purifiers. Does this count? It might not count as a classic academic replication, since I probably used different room dimensions than the original experimenters. But is it still useful to the goal of building confidence that air cleaners are useful? Maybe. </p><p>Other replication attempts have been more theoretical. Right now I\u2019ve been working with a university to check an improved method for spectroscopy prediction, and this project involves doing calculations with different approaches that should still converge to the same result in the limit. Is this exercise of value at all? </p><p>And on the other end I\u2019ve done <strong>really CHILL</strong> attempts at observing phenomena, like my experiment in heating up oil and mica powder to observe Rayleigh-Benard convection. Okay, some guy on the internet has filmed Benard convention for the thousandth time\u2026 what the marginal contribution of that?!</p><p>Right now I\u2019m just a random dude doing experiments in my house, recording it and writing about it. </p><p>On the surface, it looks <em>unserious, </em>in a conventional sens<em>e</em>. </p><p>But if you think about it, it\u2019s really unclear what counts as meaningful scientific process <strong>in general.</strong> I hope that the independent scientists amongst us think about this more clearly and take advantage of it to do unconventional things of value.</p><p></p><p></p><p></p>",
